        "orario": "17:09",
        "timestamp": 1780240198,
        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement Myanmar Asia Southeast Asia 45 killed in Myanmar’s Shan state after blast at mining explosive storage facility The village in Namhkam township is under the control of the Ta’ang National Liberation Army, an ethnic armed group opposed to the junta 2 -MIN READ 2 -MIN Listen Associated Press Published: 11:09pm, 31 May 2026 A blast on Sunday at a building in northeastern Myanmar said to have been storing explosives for mining has killed more than 45 people, according to rescuers and independent media reports. About 70 other people were injured in the explosion that took place around noon in the village of Kaungtup, in Namhkam township. The area, located about 3 kilometres (2 miles) south of the Chinese border, is under the control of the Ta’ang National Liberation Army, an ethnic armed group which has engaged in sporadic fighting against Myanmar’s central government. Advertisement A rescuer who rushed to the site of the blast told reporters that 46 bodies, including six children, had been recovered by Sunday evening and taken for cremation. The rescuer, who spoke on condition of anonymity for security reasons, said 74 injured people had been transported to the township hospital and rescue operations were continuing. Advertisement Another rescuer in Namhkam, who also spoke on condition of anonymity, said about 40 people were killed and more than 100 houses near the blast site were damaged. Myanmar media outlets, including Shan State’s online Shwe Phee Myay news agency, reported death tolls ranging from 50 to 55. They published photos and videos showing smoke from the explosion and damaged buildings and debris in its aftermath. Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
